n towns to enter Islam as mawali, only to be forced back by the Umayyad governor al-Hajjaj who refused to recognise their claims.” “The problem for the Umayyads was that they had come to power as leaders of a conquering Arab elite and to have allowed the conquered peoples to enter Islam en masse would have abolished or at least weakened the distinction between the elite and the masses. “As a result the Fitna has often been interpreted as the climax of a struggle for power within Islam between that class of Meccans typified by the Umayyads, the wealthy and powerful leaders of pre-Islamic Mecca, and those, largely from a lower social stratum, whose acceptance of Islam was more wholehearted. To use expressions frequently applied, it was the result of a struggle between the old and the new aristocracy.”'}], {'usage.total_tokens': 2011, 'temperature': 0.2})
